WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.040 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Mike McGovern an assistant professor  of anthropology at Yale University  professor McGovern is a political  anthropologist who works in West Africa  and uses a variety of sources from  kinship idioms to the aesthetics of  state-sponsored folklore to try to  understand post-colonial states within  the arc of longer historical  trajectories today we'll talk with him  about his first book entitled making war  in Cote d'Ivoire  welcome professor McGovern thanks for  having me let's begin by talking about  your book give us an overview well it's  about making war as a process not as an  event which is meant to put the process  of war making into the the broader  context of peacetime politics and so not  to see it as a rupture from peacetime  but actually see the ways in which it's  connected with the way that people do  politics in a particular place during  war and during peace and what are some  of the questions you address in your  book well you may have read or seen in  the news that Cote d'Ivoire is supposed  to be a conflict that's about religion  muslims and christians or about ethnic  groups and one of the first things i try  to do in the book is to challenge that  approach to seeing the the root causes  of the conflict in fact it's got a much  more complex set of causes which are  largely political economic ones  there's a lot of money at stake and in  fact this situation that we have in Cote  d'Ivoire which is kind of one of neither  peace nor war is very lucrative because  in having a state of emergency in the  country one can justify all kinds of  actions that allow for illegal economic  activities to take place and what drew  you to this part of the world what gave  you the idea  for the book well I was a Peace Corps  volunteer in Guinea the neighbouring  country in 1989 for two years and I was  right on the border with Liberia and  Cote d'Ivoire  and during the time I was there the war  the civil war in Liberia began and  refugees started pouring into Guinea  from there and a little bit later I went  to Cote d'Ivoire for the first time and  it was interesting because both  countries Guinea and Cote d'Ivoire had  French colonial legacies they spoke  French as a national language a lot of  things were the same and yet the  national cultures were radically  different Guinea had been a socialist  country had rejected France totally in  1958  whereas Cote d'Ivoire had actually sort  of embraced its relationship with France  even after independence the Guinean said  well the alarians look down on us  because we're poorer than they are now  but we'll see over time whether or not  the chickens come home to roost as it  were and in fact that's what happened  you know about 15 20 years later and  part of my interest is trying to  understand why that happened and how did  you do the research  what was your methodology well as I said  I had lived for a long time in Guinea  and I did my PhD dissertation on Guinea  again right near the border with Cote  d'Ivoire that research which sort of  informs my understanding of avoir Ian's  rural realities was very much done in  little villages no electricity or  running water no cell phones or Internet  and the ways that people actually made a  living as rice farmers and farming cash  crops like cocoa and coffee and the way  that they related to the the bigger  state in Guinea as in Cote d'Ivoire  there were inter-ethnic tensions having  to do with land ownership and land use  but things played out quite quite  differently in Cote d'Ivoire  so when I began going to Cote d'Ivoire  one of the things I looked at was you  know why those differences took place  later after doing my PhD I was the West  Africa director of International Crisis  Group and during that time I was  actually doing active research in Cote  d'Ivoire for about a two and a half year  period on and off I would go there I  would also go to some of the other  countries in West Africa and that's  where I did most of the research a lot  of it was in Abidjan with political  elites because of the work I was doing I  came to know people like Laurent Gbagbo  the president or ex president depending  how you see it and unless on watch era  who was elected in in November as  president but hasn't really taken power  yet as well as a lot of the other key  actors I also got to know people like  the young Patriots this movement of  young men who kind of controlled the  streets at certain moments and have very  kind of a militant violent street  politics in favor of Laurent Gbagbo so  my research was kind of mixed between  this rural village based research and  then this research with political elites  and also with ordinary people in the the  main city so basically it's talking to  people mainly talking to people but of  course I I don't I didn't do  quantitative research of my own but I  use other people's quantitative research  for instance on the economy around cocoa  as you may know 40% of the world's cocoa  comes from Cote d'Ivoire so if you had a  chocolate bar today it was probably made  with of Lorien cocoa there are calls  right now to to have an embargo on avoir  en cocoa in order to force Laurent  Gbagbo out of power so I use other  people's quantitative research to  supplement my own okay is it a dangerous  place today to be in  danger is always a relative thing I've  done research in in Liberia right after  the end of the Civil War which was much  more dangerous you know as we saw with  the bombing in the airport in Moscow  yesterday it can be dangerous to be in  lots of places but it's it's not a  politically stable country right now  particularly in the wake of these  elections from November 2010 there's  been a lot of street violence there have  been people getting disappeared and  there have been extrajudicial killings  at least allegations of torture so it's  it's an unsettled place to say the least  let's talk about some of the findings in  your book well one of the findings as I  said is that it can be very lucrative to  have this unstable situation of neither  war nor peace but one of my other  interests is how do you get to a  situation where a war seems to be taking  off and and ready to happen and yet it  never completely takes off so it's a  kind of a frozen conflict as some people  would say what are the reasons why you  get in Rwanda for instance a conflict  that has inter-ethnic conflict overtones  that really becomes a genocide where's  in Cote d'Ivoire it never gets to that  level or it never gets the level of a  full-blown war you have isolated  massacres and then long periods of peace  in between and one of the things that I  suggest is that the actors for instance  at the village level of village chief  who might be interested in reclaiming  land that had been sold to people who  came from the outside a youth politics  leader like these young Patriots who's a  university graduate but has gotten sort  of routed toward a kind of a mafia like  illegal economy because there are no  jobs for university graduates and in  Cote d'Ivoire and a political elite like  the people who are at the head  the parties they have very different  incentives interests and the part of the  question is whether or not those  interests ever get aligned with one  another and so part of what I look at in  cote ivoire is why the alignment failed  to happen to bring all of these people's  interests together so that they would  all push in one direction what you see  is actually people pulling in opposite  direction and what are the parties who  are involved in in the conflict well one  of the most interesting groups is these  young Patriots and a lot of them in fact  the two main protagonists at the youth  level are a man called Charlie geudae  who is known as the general of the  streets and he's the head of the young  Patriots and then on the opposite side  Guillaume Soho who was the  secretary-general of the new the new  forces the force nouvelle who was the  the group of X rebels controlling the  north  now Charlie geudae is a minister in the  Gbagbo government and gihon soro is  prime minister of the opposed what at  our government but it so happens that  these guys used to be roommates  they were both English majors at the  university of Abidjan they were both  leaders of the student union one after  the other during this period when the  student union was becoming criminalized  and was basically doing things like  controlling the stock of dormitories for  university students and charging people  a premium if they wanted to get access  to them and so they rather than studying  and rather than pursuing their degrees  they actually got involved in this kind  of violent student politics that had  much more to do with economics than with  studying and they themselves talked  about the way in which this was their  training for politics in kochevar they  see the people ahead of them the  generation that includes what era and  bug  as being the people who blocked their  normal progress through education toward  ordinary jobs but by the same time by  the same token those older people are  their patrons and the people who have  opened up these other possibilities  through the kind of route of political  violence so what do you foresee  happening do you think there will become  a full blown out war do you think things  will settle down with a new  administration perhaps what do you think  the future holds it's it's very tricky  there are two administrations there's  two parallel governments right now and  you have the West African regional body  threatening to intervene by military  force if Gbagbo doesn't leave and at the  same time you have this threat of  sanctions and embargoes on tiwari and  cocoa which are going to hurt ordinary  of lorian's as well as the elites so in  the the situation has become much more  fragile in the last three months I would  have said three months ago very unlikely  that you would have a full-blown war but  at this point I think it can't be ruled  out I say conclusions in your book well  the conclusion is largely that we have  to look at violence in its longer-term  historical context to understand the  reasons why people resort to it whether  they be young people or political elites  and in understanding that we might have  a better understanding of how one might  intervene from the outside to help stave  off the possibility of a full-blown war  in the process of doing the research did  you come across any particular  difficulties or surprises  well difficulties I you know yes of  course yeah and you know at a certain  point I was arrested by these young  Patriots along with the various security  forces at just a roadblock in  the western part of the country and what  happened well I my colleague and I were  held for about 12 hours interrogated  brought to another town where we were  interrogated again by the head of the  military base there who decided finally  to let us go but tell us to be gone by  by the next morning leave the country  leave that region of the of the country  and go to the capital is there any  physical violence were you in fear of  your life well yeah I mean people were  pointing guns at us but that we were it  harmed in any way  so does that color your your perspective  of the country in terms of you know how  you portray it in your writing well I  would say this when I first had a kind  of a nasty interaction with the Iranian  security forces this is about 10 years  ago I rolled into the town that I was  trying to get to and got there very late  at night found a restaurant that was  open hadn't eaten and I started  complaining to the waiter and he said  well you know I'm from the next country  over I'm from burkina faso  and all that's happened since the  soldiers took over in a coup d'etat this  was around 2000 he said all that's  changed since the soldiers took over is  that they now do the same things to  people like you that they've been doing  to me for a decade or more and it gave  me a real insight into the kinds of  grievances that a lot of people in Cote  d'Ivoire had and I've seen in shared  taxis and so forth lots of people get  hassled right up to the point of having  their papers their identity papers taken  away from them where the soldiers said  you either pay us a lot of money or you  don't get them back Wow  so in the events cote ivoire does go to  war what do you think will happen well  who do you think will win honestly I  want to yeah it's not it's not a win  situation for anybody what we've seen so  far is that it's civilians who've come  in for the worst abuse by all sides so  it's not if anybody wins it's a few  political elites who get a bigger piece  of the economic pie but for Cote  d'Ivoire as a country it's not a winning  bargain by any means well I guess we'll  see what the future holds for that  country thank you very much for being  here with us today and sharing some of  your work thanks for having me for more  information about Professor McGovern and  his work please visit our website at  yale.edu backslash McMillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the McMillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty McMillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

